17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 4"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",16," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"OS Structures & Architectures"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"How the kernel is organized inside, and the trade-offs behind every choice"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"By now you know what a kernel does: it manages the CPU, memory, and devices, and it guards the boundary between user space and hardware. But a kernel is not a single tidy function, it is millions of lines of code covering the scheduler, the memory manager, dozens of file systems, and hundreds of device drivers. All of that has to live somewhere and talk to itself somehow. How you arrange those pieces is a genuine engineering decision, and different operating systems answer it in strikingly different ways."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"This chapter is about internal architecture: the shapes a kernel can take, and why. We will look at monolithic kernels, layered designs, microkernels, and the pragmatic hybrids most real systems ship today. Along the way one theme keeps returning, you are constantly trading raw speed against reliability, security, and how easy the thing is to change without breaking it."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"If you build software for a living, this is not abstract trivia. The same tensions that shape a kernel, keep everything in one fast process, or split it into isolated services that message each other, are the exact tensions you face when you decide between a monolith and microservices. The kernel designers just had to answer these questions decades earlier, with the whole machine on the line."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-organizing-a-kernel",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-organizing-a-kernel","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"The core problem: how do you organize a kernel?"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-mechanism-vs-policy",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-mechanism-vs-policy","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"02"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Mechanism vs policy"}]]}]}],["$","li","03-monolithic-kernels",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#03-monolithic-kernels","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":["$L21","$L22"]}]}],"$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26"]}]]}],"$L27"]}],"$L28"]}]
