27:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-organizing-a-kernel",{"id":"01-organizing-a-kernel","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The core problem: how do you organize a kernel?"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A kernel has to do a great many different jobs, and every one of them is critical. It schedules threads onto CPUs, hands out and reclaims memory, drives the disk and the network card, implements the file systems, and answers every system call your programs make. None of these can simply be left out, and all of them need to run with hardware privilege. The question is not what goes in a kernel, but how those parts should be arranged relative to one another."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That arrangement matters because the pieces constantly call each other. When you write a file, the file system asks the memory manager for buffers and hands data to a disk driver. If those components sit together in one address space, the calls between them are as cheap as an ordinary function call. If they are separated into isolated protected units, every such interaction becomes a guarded message that crosses a boundary, safer, but slower. The whole chapter lives in that one trade-off."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Kernel structure"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"How the kernel’s internal components are grouped, isolated, and allowed to talk to each other."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Address space"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A protected memory region; code in one space cannot freely touch another’s memory."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Trusted computing base"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The code running with full privilege that you must trust completely, you want it small."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The tension",": "]}],"Every kernel design is a bet on how to balance three things that pull against each other: performance, reliability, and maintainability."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L29",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch04%2F01-organizing-a-kernel.jpg?v=b49189ed4cd17a82f378f69e1e138083","alt":"Handwritten notes: a big box labelled KERNEL crammed with scheduler, memory, drivers and file systems, with a question mark asking how to arrange them, flanked by three axes labelled speed, reliability and maintainability."}],"$L2a"]}]]}]]}],"$L2b","$L2c","$L2d","$L2e","$L2f","$L30"]}]
28:["$","$L31",null,{"prev":{"slug":"hardware-an-os-drives","title":"The Hardware an OS Drives","kind":"$undefined","number":3},"next":{"slug":"the-boot-process","title":"The Boot Process","kind":"$undefined","number":5}}]
2a:["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]
2b:["$","section","02-mechanism-vs-policy",{"id":"02-mechanism-vs-policy","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Mechanism vs policy"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Before comparing kernel shapes, learn the design principle that underlies all of them: separate mechanism from policy. A mechanism is the machinery that makes something possible, how it is done. A policy is the decision about what to do and when. The classic rule of thumb is that mechanisms should be flexible and general, while policies should be easy to change without rewriting the machinery underneath."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Scheduling is the cleanest example. The mechanism is everything needed to actually switch the CPU from one thread to another, saving registers, setting a timer, restoring the next thread’s state. The policy is the rule that decides which thread to run next, round-robin, priority-based, or something fairer. A well-built kernel lets you swap the policy (Linux, for instance, offers several scheduling classes) while the context-switch mechanism stays untouched."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Mechanism"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The reusable machinery that performs an action, the “how”. Ideally general and stable."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Policy"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The decision about what to do and when, the “what/when”. Ideally swappable."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Separation"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Keeping the two apart so policies can change without disturbing the mechanism."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["For engineers",": "]}],"This is the same instinct behind a clean API: expose a general mechanism (a payment gateway call) and let callers plug in policy (which provider, what retry rule). You have been applying this principle without naming it."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L29",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch04%2F02-mechanism-vs-policy.jpg?v=3f3c1f50f92922be4dce5be89216df8c","alt":"Handwritten diagram: a box labelled MECHANISM (how, the timer, context-switch machinery) sitting below a swappable box labelled POLICY (what/when, the scheduling rule), with an arrow showing the policy can be replaced without touching the mechanism."}],"$L32"]}]]}]]}]
2c:["$","section","03-monolithic-kernels",{"id":"03-monolithic-kernels","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Monolithic kernels"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The oldest and most common design is the monolithic kernel: put essentially everything, scheduler, memory manager, file systems, network stack, and device drivers, into one large program running in a single privileged address space. Because all the pieces share that space, they call each other directly, with nothing but a function call between the file system and the disk driver. That directness makes monolithic kernels very fast."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The cost is isolation, or the lack of it. Since everything runs with full privilege in one space, a bug anywhere can corrupt anything. A flaw in an obscure device driver can overwrite the scheduler’s data and bring the entire machine down, because there is no wall between them. The codebase is also large and tightly interwoven, which historically made monolithic kernels harder to reason about and extend."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Linux is the headline example, and it is important to be precise: Linux is a monolithic kernel, but a modular one. All the core subsystems live together in kernel space for speed, yet drivers and features can be compiled as loadable modules and slotted in at runtime. That modularity tames the maintainability problem without giving up the performance of the shared address space, we will come back to modules shortly."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Single address space"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"All kernel components share one protected memory region and call each other directly."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Fast path"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Cross-component calls are ordinary function calls, no boundary crossing, no message."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Blast radius"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A bug in any component can corrupt the whole kernel; there is no internal wall."}]]}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Real systems",": "]}],"Linux and the classic Unix kernels are monolithic. It is the design that runs most servers, phones, and cloud workloads on Earth."]}]]}]]}],"$L33"]}]]}]
2d:["$","section","04-layered-systems",{"id":"04-layered-systems","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Layered systems"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A layered system tackles the tangle of a big kernel by imposing strict order: organize the OS into a stack of layers, where each layer may only use the services of the layer directly below it and offers services to the layer directly above. Layer 0 is the hardware; the top layer is the user interface. This is the classic textbook structure, and its appeal is real, each layer can be built and understood using only what is beneath it, so you can reason about and debug the system one level at a time."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The difficulty is that real operating systems do not decompose cleanly into a neat pile of layers. Deciding what belongs in which layer is genuinely hard, because subsystems often need each other in ways that cross layer boundaries, the memory manager may need the disk driver to page data out, while the disk driver may need the memory manager for its buffers. Strict layering also adds cost: a single request may have to pass down through many layers, each adding a little overhead, which can make the system slow compared with a design that just calls across directly."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Layer"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A group of functionality that uses only the layer below and serves only the layer above."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Clean dependency"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Each layer’s correctness depends only on the layers beneath it, easy to reason about."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Rigidity"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Real subsystems have circular needs that a strict layer stack cannot express cleanly."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Where you see it",": "]}],"Pure layering is rare in whole kernels, but the idea thrives in the networking stack (the OSI/TCP-IP layers) and in how you structure application software."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L29",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch04%2F04-layered-systems.jpg?v=7368d52ef7b3d29c2499db9a161ecd5e","alt":"Handwritten diagram: a stack of horizontal layers numbered from the hardware at the bottom up to user programs at the top, each layer with an arrow showing it may only call the layer directly beneath it."}],"$L34"]}]]}]]}]
2e:["$","section","05-microkernels",{"id":"05-microkernels","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Microkernels"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A microkernel takes the opposite bet from a monolith: keep the kernel as small as possible. Only the truly essential functions stay in privileged kernel space, inter-process communication (IPC), basic scheduling, and low-level memory management. Everything else that a monolith would keep inside the kernel, device drivers, file systems, the network stack, is pushed out to run as ordinary user-space processes called servers."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Those servers cannot call each other directly anymore, because each lives in its own address space. Instead they communicate by message passing, relayed through the microkernel. When an application wants a file, it sends a message to the file-system server, which may send a message to the disk-driver server, and replies flow back the same way. The kernel’s main job shrinks to being a fast, secure post office for these messages."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The payoff is robustness and security. If a device driver crashes, only its server dies, the kernel can often just restart it while the rest of the system keeps running, because the faulty driver never had the privilege to corrupt anything else. The trusted computing base is tiny, which is why microkernels dominate where correctness is non-negotiable. The cost is IPC overhead: all that message passing across address-space boundaries is slower than a monolith’s direct calls, and taming that overhead has been the central challenge of microkernel research."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Microkernel"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A minimal kernel holding only IPC, scheduling, and basic memory management."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"User-space server"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A driver or file system running as an unprivileged process outside the kernel."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Message passing"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The IPC by which servers and apps request work from one another through the kernel."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Fault isolation"}],"$L35"]}]]}],"$L36"]}],"$L37"]}]]}]
2f:["$","section","06-modules-and-hybrid",{"id":"06-modules-and-hybrid","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Modules & hybrid kernels"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Most real systems refuse to pick a pure extreme, and instead land in a pragmatic middle. The first tool for that is the loadable kernel module: a chunk of kernel code, usually a driver or a file system, that can be loaded into and unloaded from a running monolithic kernel on demand. Linux uses this heavily. You get the speed of a shared address space, but you no longer ship one giant fixed binary; you load exactly the drivers your hardware needs, and update them without rebuilding the whole kernel."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The second is the hybrid kernel, which borrows the vocabulary of microkernels while keeping performance-critical services inside kernel space. A hybrid design is organized into components that could, in principle, be separate servers, but the parts that would suffer most from IPC overhead, like drivers and file systems, are kept in the kernel and called directly. It is a deliberate compromise: some of the modularity and structure of a microkernel, most of the speed of a monolith."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is what you are almost certainly running. Windows NT (the basis of every modern Windows) is a hybrid kernel. Apple’s XNU, at the heart of macOS and iOS, famously combines a Mach-derived microkernel with a monolithic BSD layer in a single kernel space. These systems chose the middle path because, in practice, pure purity on either end costs more than it is worth."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Loadable module"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Kernel code loaded/unloaded at runtime; adds features without rebuilding the kernel."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Hybrid kernel"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A kernel structured with microkernel ideas but keeping key services in kernel space for speed."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Pragmatic compromise"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Trading a little of one extreme’s purity to capture most of the other’s benefit."}]]}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],"$L38"]}]]}],"$L39"]}]]}]
30:["$","section","07-comparing-approaches",{"id":"07-comparing-approaches","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Comparing the approaches & why it matters to you"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Line the three main approaches up and the trade-off becomes concrete. A monolithic kernel is fastest, because components call each other directly, but least robust, because a single bug has the run of the whole kernel. A microkernel is most robust and most secure, because faults are isolated in user space and the trusted core is tiny, but pays for it with IPC overhead. A hybrid deliberately sits in between, keeping the hot paths in-kernel for speed while borrowing structure from the microkernel world."}],["$","dl","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Monolithic, speed"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Highest: direct in-kernel calls, no boundary crossings. Robustness lower; example: Linux."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Microkernel, robustness"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Highest: isolated servers, tiny trusted base. Speed lower from IPC; examples: QNX, seL4."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Hybrid, balance"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Near-monolith speed with some modularity; examples: Windows NT, macOS/iOS (XNU)."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Complexity"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Monoliths are simple to call but hard to isolate; microkernels invert that."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Here is where it touches you directly. On a monolithic system, a faulty graphics or storage driver runs with full kernel privilege, so a bad enough bug can freeze or crash the entire machine, the Windows blue screen and the Linux kernel panic are exactly this. On a microkernel system like QNX, that same driver runs as an isolated user-space server, so when it dies the OS can restart it and the machine stays up. That is precisely why microkernels are chosen for cars, aircraft, and medical devices, where a crash is not a mere inconvenience."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Notice how closely this mirrors your own architectural choices. A monolithic kernel is a monolith app: fast internal calls, simple to build, but one bad module can take the whole thing down. A microkernel is microservices: isolated processes talking by messages, resilient to a single failure, but paying a real cost in the communication between them. The kernel world has been living this debate for forty years, and the industry-wide answer, hybrids, modular monoliths, sensible middles, is the same lesson your own systems keep teaching you."}],"$L3a"]}],"$L3b"]}]]}]
