35:["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A crashing server can be restarted without taking down the kernel or other servers."}]
36:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Real systems",": "]}],"MINIX (built to teach these ideas), QNX (real-time, in cars and medical devices), and seL4 (formally proven correct) are microkernels. seL4 is used where a bug is unacceptable."]}]]}]
37:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L29",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch04%2F05-microkernels.jpg?v=1e20d30333a170e1ebb69847fff85fd0","alt":"Handwritten notes: a tiny box labelled MICROKERNEL (only IPC, scheduling, basic memory) in kernel space, with file-system, driver and network servers drawn as separate boxes up in user space, message arrows passing between them through the kernel."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
38:["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Real systems",": "]}],"Windows NT is a hybrid; macOS/iOS run on XNU (Mach + BSD). Linux is a modular monolith. The middle ground is where the mainstream lives."]}]
39:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L29",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch04%2F06-modules-and-hybrid.jpg?v=2972a31a0d554b8f4aed7ee21256ced1","alt":"Handwritten diagram: on the left a monolithic core with loadable module blocks snapping into it at runtime; on the right a hybrid kernel box that keeps drivers and file systems inside kernel space but is organized into microkernel-style components."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3a:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Looking ahead",": "]}],"We have seen how the kernel is shaped from the outside in. Next we go inside one of its central jobs, the process: how a program becomes a running process, and how the OS keeps track of it."]}]]}]
